    links: [
        { to: 'theban', relation: 'Printed in the Polygraphia (July 1518), where Trithemius ascribes it to Honorius surnamed the Theban on Pietro d’Abano’s testimony. Agrippa reprinted it in III.29 (1533) with the same ascription, naming Peter of Abano but not Trithemius.' },
        { to: 'latin', relation: 'Both alphabets treated here, the Theban and the cipher of the Golden Dawn’s manuscripts, are keyed to the Latin letters. The Latin hall’s timeline carries the Steganographia, and its reading list the Cipher Manuscripts.' },
        { to: 'agrippa', relation: 'Agrippa stayed with Trithemius at Würzburg and sent him the draft of De occulta philosophia; the English of 1651 prints both their letters in its front matter, and III.24 cites the abbot’s treatise to Maximilian.' },
        { to: 'the-books', relation: 'The chain of books through which the alphabets travelled, with the Polygraphia of 1518 near its head.' },
        { to: 'celestial', relation: 'Agrippa III.30. The draft of 1510 went to Trithemius, but whether it already held this alphabet is not known.' },
        { to: 'transitus-fluvii', relation: 'The third alphabet of Agrippa III.30, with the same open question about the draft.' },
        { to: 'malachim', relation: 'A contrast: the Golden Dawn’s Cipher Manuscripts are written in Trithemius’s Latin-keyed cipher, not in this Hebrew-keyed script.' },
        { to: 'aiq-bekar', relation: 'Another cipher the Golden Dawn used, for sigils; its Cipher Manuscripts are in Trithemius’s alphabet, not in the nine chambers.' },
        { to: 'enochian', relation: 'The Golden Dawn, whose founding manuscripts are in Trithemius’s cipher, took up Dee’s angelic alphabet as well.' },
        { to: 'charakteres', relation: 'Invented signs are far older than Trithemius. He printed a catalogue of secret alphabets; he did not begin the practice.' },
    ],
    cautions: [
        'Trithemius ascribes the Theban to Honorius surnamed the Theban, citing Pietro d’Abano; that is his attribution, and Peterson could find no such passage in d’Abano. Agrippa repeats the ascription without naming Trithemius. Neither says who made the signs.',
        'Agrippa is not documented as Trithemius’s pupil. The record is a stay at Würzburg in the winter of 1509–10 and the letters exchanged over the draft, the reply dated 8 April 1510. The draft was sent “by this bearer”, by Trithemius’s account. Whether it held any of the secret alphabets is not known.',
        'The Steganographia is given here in both its readings, as a cipher manual and as a book of angel magic. The page endorses neither.',
        'The dates are easily confused. The Steganographia was begun in 1499 and printed in 1606; the Polygraphia was written about 1508, presented in manuscript to Maximilian, and printed in July 1518. Neither was printed in its author’s lifetime.',
        '“Ave Maria cipher” is a later name for the Polygraphia’s word tables, and it is not given here as Trithemius’s own title for them.',
        'The 1518 printing is unpaginated. The page numbers cited for its alphabets, 579 by Peterson and 597 by Wikipedia, cannot be pages of it and fit a later paginated edition; and the book that holds the catalogue is given as VI by Peterson and as V in reports not yet confirmed.',
        'The printer and place of the 1518 Polygraphia are disputed: Michael Furter or Adam Petri at Basel, or Jakob Köbel at Oppenheim; the Folger records Furter’s death in the spring of 1517. The Metropolitan Museum gives the woodcuts to Hans Springinklee; the title woodcut is his by Dodgson’s attribution and the work of an artist of Burgkmair’s workshop by M. Lankorońska’s, as the Folger records.',
        'No sign of the cipher alphabet is shown, and none will be drawn from a modern font, a cipher website or a chart that names no edition. Its register is provisional until the key of the Cipher Manuscripts has been read.',
        'The rituals of the Cipher Manuscripts, and the Golden Dawn ceremonies worked up from them, are not reproduced: living orders use them. Westcott’s account of Anna Sprengel is reported as his account, and the conclusion that he forged her letters as Howe’s and Gilbert’s.',
        'J.F.’s English of 1651 calls Trithemius “a Neapolitanian Abbot”. That stands for Peapolitanus, his title at Würzburg; the same front matter heads his letter “Abbot of Saint James of Herbipolis” and dates it from “our Monastery of Peapolis”.',
        'The seven intelligences of III.24 are spelled as J.F. prints them: Oriphiel, Zachariel, Zamael, Michael, Anael, Raphael, Gabriel.',
        'The story that Trithemius raised the shade of Mary of Burgundy for Maximilian is a legend. It is left out of the record here, since the writer who first told it has not been identified.',
    ],
